Changes in development version
 * tsggplotly: plotly is now a suggested rather than a required dependency; tsggplotly() asks for it to be installed when it is missing
 * tsggplot: fixed the line-centering shift (line_to_middle) for a daily/weekly/yearly/hourly xts series being computed from the unreliable frequency() instead of the series' own actual spacing -- only visibly wrong for hourly data (plotted on a real datetime axis with no day-level rounding to absorb it), but now correct throughout (#4)
 * tsggplotly: the interactive plot's legend is now actually split left/right for a tsr chart, matching the static plot, instead of always falling back to one merged legend (#16)
 * tsggplot: fixed a CI's shaded band silently disappearing (or, with CIs on both axes, both taking on the same colours) when combined with left_as_band/left_as_bar or a second-axis CI -- each used to add its own fill scale, and ggplot2 only keeps the last one added (#14)
 * tsggplot: with fill_year_with_nas = TRUE the trailing x margin now defaults to none (the filled-up year already ends the axis, like tsplot); an explicit axis_x_pad still applies
 * tsggplot: highlight_window_alpha is now the actual opacity of the highlight window (default 0.55); it was previously mapped inside aes() and so had no effect on the value, so an explicitly set value now looks different
 * tsggplot: the sum line of a stacked bar chart gets its own legend entry, grouped with the left-axis series when a tsr series is present
 * tsggplotly: the highlight window is drawn as a shape below the traces, and only that layer is taken out of the plot (other geom_rect() layers are kept)
 * tsggplotly: keeps the subtitle (as a line of the title, hidden when the theme hides it) and makes room for multi-line titles
 * tsggplot: daily/weekly xts series with fill_year_with_nas keep the closing year tick on the axis in time zones ahead of UTC
 * tsggplotly: the axes are no longer titled in the interactive plot (series are named by the legend and hover text); new axis_titles = TRUE argument shows the titles set through labs
 * tsggplot: setting only labs$y_right no longer makes a stray "value" title appear on the left axis (labs$y partial-matched y_right), in the static and the interactive plot
 * tsggplot: added support for zoo objects
 * tsggplot: added support for yearly and hourly xts frequencies, verified weekly/daily
 * tsggplot: fixed x-position handling for xts series so plotted data lines up with axis ticks (affected yearly/hourly/monthly/quarterly xts)
 * tsggplot: fixed line_to_middle centering for xts series (frequency() was unreliable, e.g. near-zero for hourly data)
 * tsggplotly: rewritten with working custom hover text and a real secondary y-axis (built from the same axis metadata used to draw the plot); removed dead commented-out code
 * tsggplot: quiet argument now wired through consistently (returns axis metadata alongside the plot when FALSE), matching tsplot's behavior
 * tsggplot: fixed overlapping yearly x-axis labels on longer series / narrower plots
 * tsggplotly: fixed overlapping yearly x-axis labels in the interactive plot too (the static fix above doesn't carry over automatically); new x_tick_mode argument ("thin"/"auto") lets you choose between our own overlap-avoidance or handing tick placement to Plotly
 * tsggplotly: interactive plot now follows the theme passed to tsggplot() instead of hardcoded styling -- font family, plot/panel background colour, and legend position/orientation/visibility (including legend.position = "none") all carry over; "thin" mode's tick marks are now drawn at the true year-start positions (matching the static plot's minor ticks) instead of the centered label positions
 * tsggplot: when a tsr (right-axis) series is present, its legend is now grouped separately from the left-axis legend below the plot by default, matching tsplot; pass legend_all_left = TRUE (new theme parameter) to merge them back into one legend, as before. Static (print/save) output only -- tsggplotly() always renders a single merged legend regardless (it converts the merged-legend version transparently). New dependency: ggnewscale
 * tsggplotly: x_tick_mode = "auto" now gives Plotly a real date-typed x-axis (reconstructed from tsggplot()'s numeric x) instead of a plain numeric one, so zooming in shows properly formatted month/day tick labels instead of decimal years like 2020.5
 * tsggplot: the margin left after the last data point now scales with the series' own span instead of always being a fixed ~one-quarter-year margin, so a short daily/hourly series isn't dwarfed by several months of empty axis; new axis_x_pad theme parameter lets you set it explicitly (0 for none) instead of relying on the automatic scaling
 * tsggplot: daily/weekly xts series now pick sensible day/week/month/year x-axis tick spacing based on their own span by default, instead of always being year-spaced (which produced a broken NA tick for anything shorter than a year); axis_x_date_ticks = "years" theme parameter restores the old fixed year-based spacing
 * tsggplot: fixed a crash/blank-axis case where a series short enough to have only one yearly tick in its range showed no x-axis ticks or labels at all
 * tsggplot: hourly xts series now plot on a real datetime x-axis (scale_x_datetime), like daily/weekly's date axis, instead of the numeric decimal-year axis used for monthly/quarterly/ts data -- gets real hour/day/month tick labels (also respects axis_x_date_ticks) instead of decimal years like 2023.5; tsggplotly()'s x_tick_mode = "auto" reconstructs real datetimes for hourly series too
 * tsggplot: highlight_window_start/highlight_window_end now accept a Date/POSIXct value (or a "YYYY-MM-DD" string) directly for daily/weekly/hourly series, instead of only the ts-style c(year, period) + highlight_window_freq convention, which doesn't apply to those and silently produced a wrongly-positioned/invisible highlight; the default highlight (no start/end given) also now correctly spans ~2 years on those series instead of ~2 days or ~2 seconds
 * tsggplotly: no longer requires legend_all_left = TRUE for a tsr plot -- it now converts the merged-legend equivalent automatically instead of erroring
 * tsggplotly: fixed the secondary (right) y-axis not actually rendering at all in the interactive plot -- ticks, axis line and title were correctly present in the underlying data, but Plotly.js silently drops an axis with no trace bound to it; an invisible point is now added on that axis so it displays
 * tsggplotly: fixed the interactive plot rendering in a serif font instead of the theme's actual (sans-serif) font -- "sans"/"serif"/"mono" are R graphics-device font names, not valid CSS, so passing them straight through left the browser falling back to its own default; now mapped to a real font stack everywhere ggplotly() sets one, including axis titles and tick labels (not just the plot's overall default)
 * tsggplotly: fixed the left y-axis line rendering in black regardless of theme, and the right/secondary axis having no line at all, unlike the x-axis which was already correctly themed -- all three now consistently use the theme's actual axis.line colour/width
 * tsggplot: fixed a stray black box appearing behind a series' line in the legend when the plot also uses confidence intervals (ci) -- ggplot2 merges the CI's fill legend into the colour legend, which without an override left every colour key (including the plain line's) drawing with the fill geom's default background swatch
 * tsggplot: the left- and right-axis legends (split by default when a tsr series is present) now have visible spacing between them -- ggplot2 placed the two guide-boxes right next to each other with no gap, so they read as one continuous legend instead of the two distinct groups described in the docs
 * tsggplotly: fixed tick label font size being inconsistent across the left, right and x axes (ggplotly()'s own font-size detection is unreliable and could be off by 50% or more between axes on an identically-themed plot) and axis/legend text rendering in the wrong colour (ggplot2's actual inherited default, e.g. axis text's grey30, wasn't resolved -- only explicitly-set theme values were) -- both now read the theme directly, the same way axis line styling already did
 * tsggplotly: now warns if the theme's font family is a single font name with no CSS fallback stack (e.g. "Comic Sans MS" instead of "Comic Sans MS, cursive") -- if that font isn't installed in the viewer's browser, Plotly silently substitutes its own default instead of erroring, so this flags it up front instead of leaving it to be noticed by eye
 * tsggplotly: fixed x_tick_mode = "auto" not actually showing any tick marks -- only the labels moved/reformatted when zooming, since native tick marks were unconditionally suppressed (a leftover from "thin" mode, which draws its own instead) with nothing filling in for "auto"; tick appearance (length/width/colour) is Plotly's own default rather than derived from the static theme, since "auto" already hands tick placement/density to Plotly
 * tsggplotly: fixed x_tick_mode = "auto" ticks still not actually rendering (turning "ticks" on wasn't enough -- their length was left at 0, inherited from the static theme's zeroed-out major tick length, which draws nothing regardless) and fixed the same mode silently reverting the x-axis to a categorical scale with hundreds of tick candidates (plotly_build() treats a character-typed trace x as discrete data unless tickvals/ticktext are set, which "auto" deliberately clears) -- the trace x is now a real Date/POSIXct object instead of a formatted string, sidestepping that entirely
 * tsggplotly: fixed x_tick_mode = "auto" printing "Can't display both discrete & non-discrete data on same axis" into any document rendering a tsr plot -- the invisible marker added to force the secondary axis to render took its x position from the (by then reformatted-to-a-string) axis range instead of a real trace, creating a genuine type mismatch against the other, correctly Date-typed traces
 * tsggplotly: fixed stray, mixed-direction tick-like marks in x_tick_mode = "auto" -- tsggplot()'s "mid" x-axis label positioning draws its small quarterly ticks as an actual (if visually subtle) ggplot2 layer rather than a genuine axis element, which ggplotly() has no way to distinguish from real plotted data and converted into an ordinary, hoverable data trace. In "thin" mode those small marks between the yearly ticks are the intended look, so they are now redrawn as plain shapes (like the yearly ticks already are, and visually unchanged); in "auto" mode Plotly places its own ticks, which the fixed quarterly positions don't line up with, so they are dropped
